Okay, so each time you made the attempt, in those circumstances,
the calculation would've been rn2(14) > 5.  According to the wiki,
rn2(n) returns an integer from 0 to n-1, unweighted (i.e., each
outcome is equally likely.  In this case, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 are
not > 5, while 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, and 13 are, i.e., on each
attempt the odds of success were very slightly more than 57%,
and the odds of failure round to 43%.

(Incidentally, the wiki gives the conversion success formula
   ((XL + 2) / (XL + 8)),
but in your case at XL6 that would be (8/14) chance of
success, implying 6/14 of failure, which is exactly equivalent
to the formula you gave, at least for the case where XL is 6.)

In aggregate, on multiple attempts...
The odds of failing twice in a row would be (6/14)^2 > 18%
The odds of failing thrice in a row would be (6/14)^3 > 7.8%
The odds of failing four times in a row would be (6/14)^4 > 3.3%
The odds of failing five times in a row would be (6/14)^5 > 1.4%
The odds of failing six times in a row would be (6/14)^6 > 0.6%
The odds of failing seven times in a row would be (6/14)^7 > 0.26%
The odds of failing eight times in a row would be (6/14)^8  > 0.01%

Skipping down a bit, you said luck -12, so assuming
you started at zero and hadn't done anything else to
mess up your luck...

(6/14)^12 = 0.000038395 = 0.003839535%
That's roughly 4 chances out of a hundred thousand
that this would happen, in any given game where you
attempt to convert an altar, assuming you keep trying
(rather than giving up after the third failure or so, which
is probably what I would do).

All of that assumes nothing was overtly preventing you
from succeeding, except the RNG.  For instance, we
are assuming that your alignment was OK.

Now, that does seem like pretty low odds.  But the
thing is, there are millions of such unusual things that
can happen in NetHack, *each* of which individually
has low odds like that.  In any given game, the odds
that *something* this unusual will occur are significantly
higher than four in a hundred thousand.  Individually,
it's an unusual event.  In your next game (or next
twenty games, for that matter), you're very unlikely
to see this same unusual event happen.  But very
possibly something else *different* might happen,
something similarly unusual.

So yeah, the RNG has a way of doing weird stuff
to you from time to time.  Unpredictably.

